## Middle service: identity bridge

A thin service sits between callers and Coder. Humans present an **IdP JWT**; CI presents a **service credential**. Middle validates the caller, extracts identity, ensures a Coder user exists, mints a Coder API token on their behalf, then drives workspaces — **without Coder dashboard login**.

### Sign-in: third-party JWT → middle → Coder token

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  actor User
  participant IdP as Third-party IdP
  participant UI as Product UI
  participant Mid as Middle service
  participant Coder as Coder API

  Note over Mid: Holds Owner token + IdP JWKS / shared secret<br/>Never expose Owner token to the browser

  User->>IdP: Authenticate
  IdP-->>User: JWT (email, sub, …)
  User->>UI: Connect with JWT
  UI->>Mid: API call Authorization: Bearer JWT
  Mid->>Mid: Validate JWT signature / exp / aud
  Mid->>Mid: Extract user details (e.g. email)
  Mid->>Coder: GET /users?q=email (Owner token)
  Coder-->>Mid: Coder user id
  Mid->>Coder: POST /users/{id}/keys/tokens (Owner token)
  Coder-->>Mid: Coder session_token
  Note over Mid: Keep Coder token server-side<br/>bound to middle session or request context

  Note over User,UI: User never visits Coder login / dashboard
```

### Claim → Coder user mapping

| JWT claim (example) | Middle service use |
| --- | --- |
| `email` | Look up Coder user (`GET /users?q=…`) |
| `sub` / `preferred_username` | Optional stable id / username mapping |
| `aud` / `iss` / `exp` | Reject if invalid |

If the Coder user does not exist yet, middle can **create** them with the Owner token before minting a token — product policy decision.

### CI / machine identity

| Approach | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| CI service token / mTLS / OIDC client credentials | Middle authenticates the pipeline, not a human JWT |
| Mapped Coder user | Shared automation user **or** per-pipeline bot user (open decision) |
| Same run API | CI uses `POST /v1/runs` like the UI — no Coder-specific knowledge in pipelines |

### Open decision: JWT on every call vs middle session

How the **product UI** authenticates to middle **after** first contact is not locked yet.

| Approach | Idea | Tradeoffs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **A.** Validate IdP JWT on every call | Browser sends third-party JWT on each middle request | Simple; couples every hop to IdP JWT lifetime / JWKS / refresh |
| **B.** Exchange once → middle session | Validate IdP JWT at `POST /v1/session`, then issue a middle session (opaque server-side id, or short-lived middle-signed token) bound to user + cached Coder token | Session store/keys + logout/refresh design; clearer TTL and revocation |
| **C.** Hybrid | Accept IdP JWT *or* middle session during migration | Flexible now; more paths to secure |

**Leading candidate to evaluate:** B (exchange IdP JWT for a middle session; keep Coder tokens server-side). A raw UUID is only acceptable if it is **server-bound** (user, Coder token, expiry) — not “random id in localStorage” as auth by itself.

**Research before locking:**

- How the product UI already handles IdP JWT refresh / logout  
- Stateless (middle-signed JWT) vs stateful (Redis/DB) middle sessions  
- Revocation (immediate logout vs wait for `exp`)  
- Threat model: token theft, XSS, CSRF if cookies are used  

Until that lands, treat “JWT on every call” in diagrams as **illustrative**, not the final session strategy. CI auth is separate (service credentials) and does not depend on this UI decision.

### Middle responsibilities (checklist)

1. Authenticate product callers (IdP JWT) and CI callers (service auth).
2. Map identity → Coder user (create if policy allows).
3. Hold Owner API token; mint per-user Coder tokens; keep them server-side.
4. Expose stable job/run API to UI and CI.
5. Drive Coder workspace create / watch / stop / delete.
6. Enforce policy: allowed template(s), max runtime, concurrency, who can start jobs.
7. Own run state machine and artifact references.

**v1 out of scope for this path:** PTY/terminal proxy, code-server / VS Code embed, interactive Coder dashboard for end users. (Those remain possible later as optional middle proxies; they are not required to replace Tasks.)

